<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint-Domingue</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint-Domingue was the French colony on the western part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. In the eighteenth century its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> plantations, worked by enslaved Africans, made it one of the most profitable colonies in the world. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> that began in 1791 ended French rule, and the colony became the independent state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in 1804, a major loss for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-colonial-empire">French Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-domingue.jpg" alt="Saint-Domingue" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bluebear2 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pavillon_LouisXIV.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
